Three file shapes are accepted from \code{path}: comma-delimited \code{.csv} files with camelCase column names (the
plant-side groups), pipe-delimited \code{.txt} files with snake_case column names (the animals export),
and the verbatim \code{.parquet} copies stored in a data release. Files are located by a \code{taxon}/\code{name(s)}
marker in their filename (e.g. \verb{AFL-taxon-2026-09-18.csv}, \code{AFD_export_name_20260929.txt},
\code{nsl_animals_taxon.parquet}).

The two files are combined with the taxon file always taking priority over the names file: every
name already represented in the taxon file (matched on its own \code{scientific_name_id}) is dropped from
the names file's contribution before combining, and the taxon rows are bound first, so a name never
enters the result twice. The names file can only \emph{widen} matching coverage (informal/unplaced names
with no taxon concept of their own), never resolve a synonym forward, since it carries no
\code{accepted_name_usage_id} at all; the taxon file is the one source of real synonymy here.

\code{genus} is read directly from the names file's own \code{generic_name} column, but is derived from the
\emph{raw} canonical name for the taxon file: there, \code{generic_name} (where present at all) is the genus
of the \emph{accepted} name, not of the row's own name -- wrong for a synonym sitting under a different
genus. NSL's own convention always prefixes an infrageneric/infraspecific canonical name with its
governing genus (e.g. \code{"Agaricus subg. Homophron"}, \code{"Acanthiza (Geobasileus)"},
\code{"Scutellinia badioberbis"}), so the first word recovers it for genus rank and everything narrower;
\code{genus} is \code{NA} above genus rank.

Subgenus rank gets one further correction: \code{canonical_name} is stripped down to the bare subgenus
name, from either NSL's botanical \code{"Agaricus subg. Homophron"} form or the zoological
\code{"Acanthiza (Geobasileus)"} form -- matching what taxonAlign's bracketed \code{"Genus (Subgenus)"}
matching expects \code{canonical_name} to already be.

Excluded, vagrant and intercepted taxa (statuses \code{"excluded"}, \code{"excluded name"},
\code{"excluded other"}, \code{"vagrant species"}, \code{"intercepted"}) are made self-referential: in the animals
export their \code{accepted_name_usage_id} points at the parent genus/family rather than at a synonym,
which \code{\link[taxonAlign:update_taxa]{taxonAlign::update_taxa()}} would otherwise misread as "this name is now that family".
